gfopenntlm.library/--background--               gfopenntlm.library/--background--

    PURPOSE
        NTLMv2 authentication ([MS-NLMP]) and the Credential Security
        Support Provider protocol ([MS-CSSP]) for AmigaOS 3.x on 68020 and
        up: the Network Level Authentication of an RDP client, and NTLM for
        anything else that needs it.

        MIT licensed. Copyright (c) 2026 AmiCradle Team. Written from
        Microsoft's published Open Specifications and the RFCs only (see
        README.md, "Clean room").

    CONVENTIONS
        The handles (struct GNNTLM, struct GNCredSSP) are opaque. Config
        structures begin with Size; clear them, set Size to their sizeof and
        fill in the fields. A later library accepts an older, smaller Size.

        Calls that answer a LONG answer GNERR_OK (0), GNERR_CONTINUE (1) or
        a negative GNERR_ code (libraries/gfopenntlm.h). Buffers are the
        caller's: each call takes the output buffer's size and writes the
        length it used.

        Strings are UTF-8, converted to UTF-16LE as the protocols need.
        NTOWFv2's Uppercase(User) ([MS-NLMP] 3.3.2) is the Unicode simple
        uppercase mapping for Basic Latin, Latin-1 (not U+00B5), Latin
        Extended-A (not U+0131 and U+017F, which Windows leaves alone),
        Latin Extended-B to U+0233, the IPA letters with a Latin capital,
        Greek U+0386..U+03CE, Cyrillic U+0430..U+04FF, Armenian, Latin
        Extended Additional (U+1E00..U+1E95, U+1EA0..U+1EF9) and the
        fullwidth Latin letters. Other letters (Greek Extended, Georgian,
        Cherokee, outside the BMP, ...) are left as they are, and one-to-many
        mappings (U+00DF) are not made. The domain is not upper-cased.

    CHANNEL BINDINGS (1.1)
        GNNTLMConfig and GNCredSSPConfig end with ChannelBindings and
        ChannelBindingsLength. NULL/0, or a 1.0 Size without them, is
        unbound: MsvChannelBindings is Z(16), as in 1.0. Otherwise the
        AUTHENTICATE_MESSAGE carries MD5 of a gss_channel_bindings_struct
        (RFC 2744 3.11, laid out as [MS-NLMP] 2.2.2.1 and RFC 4121 4.1.1.2
        hash it: Z(16) for the empty addresses, the 32-bit little-endian
        length of application_data, then application_data).

        With Flags GNF_CB_TLS_SERVER_END_POINT, ChannelBindings is the
        server certificate's tls-server-end-point hash (RFC 5929 4.1: the
        certificate's signature hash, SHA-256 for MD5 and SHA-1) and the
        library makes application_data "tls-server-end-point:" + hash;
        without the flag ChannelBindings is the whole application_data.
        GN_NTLMGetAttr()/GN_CredSSPGetAttr(GNA_CHANNELBINDINGS) is 1 when
        bound. A Flags bit the library does not know, bindings of length 0
        or the flag without bindings give GNERR_BADARGS.

        CredSSP binds the TLS key in pubKeyAuth either way; the NTLM
        bindings are for servers that check them (Extended Protection).

        A handle belongs to one task at a time. Separate handles can be used
        by separate tasks at once.

    RANDOM NUMBERS
        Client challenges, session keys and CredSSP nonces come from
        gfopentls.library 1's OT_Random(), opened on first use. Without it
        those calls answer GNERR_NORANDOM.

    STATIC LINKING
        Define GFOPENNTLM_STATIC before <proto/gfopenntlm.h> and link
        lib/libgfopenntlm.a: the same calls, no library base.

    SECURITY
        A password given to GN_NTLMNew() is turned into its NT hash and not
        kept. GN_CredSSPNew() keeps it (CredSSP delegates it to the server
        in TSPasswordCreds) and wipes it in GN_CredSSPFree(). Key material
        is wiped when a handle is freed. NTLM is old cryptography (MD4, MD5,
        RC4); [MS-NLMP] section 5 says more.

gfopenntlm.library/GN_Version                       gfopenntlm.library/GN_Version

    NAME
        GN_Version -- the library's API version (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        version = GN_Version()
        D0

        ULONG GN_Version(VOID);

    RESULT
        (version << 16) | revision: 0x00010000 for 1.0, 0x00010001 for 1.1
        (channel bindings, GNA_CHANNELBINDINGS).

gfopenntlm.library/GN_ErrorText                   gfopenntlm.library/GN_ErrorText

    NAME
        GN_ErrorText -- English text for a GNERR_ code (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        text = GN_ErrorText(error)
        D0                  D0

        CONST_STRPTR GN_ErrorText(LONG error);

    RESULT
        A constant string; never NULL.

gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMNew                       gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMNew

    NAME
        GN_NTLMNew -- an NTLMv2 client context (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        ntlm = GN_NTLMNew(config, error)
        D0                A0      A1

        struct GNNTLM *GN_NTLMNew(CONST struct GNNTLMConfig *config,
                                  LONG *error);

    FUNCTION
        Makes a client context for one NTLM exchange ([MS-NLMP] 3.1.5.1)
        with User, Domain, Password, Workstation and TargetName (the
        service principal name sent in MsvAvTargetName, or NULL), and from
        1.1 ChannelBindings (see CHANNEL BINDINGS).

        The client offers UNICODE, REQUEST_TARGET, SIGN, SEAL, NTLM,
        ALWAYS_SIGN, EXTENDED_SESSIONSECURITY, TARGET_INFO, VERSION, 128,
        KEY_EXCH and 56, and answers with NTLMv2 only.

    INPUTS
        config - the configuration (see CONVENTIONS)
        error  - where to put a GNERR_ code, or NULL

    RESULT
        The context, or NULL with *error set.

    SEE ALSO
        GN_NTLMFree(), GN_NTLMNegotiate()

gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMFree                     gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMFree

    NAME
        GN_NTLMFree -- free an NTLM context (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        GN_NTLMFree(ntlm)
                    A0

        VOID GN_NTLMFree(struct GNNTLM *ntlm);

    FUNCTION
        Wipes the context's keys and frees it. NULL is allowed.

gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMNegotiate           gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMNegotiate

    NAME
        GN_NTLMNegotiate -- the NEGOTIATE_MESSAGE (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        result = GN_NTLMNegotiate(ntlm, out, size, length)
        D0                        A0    A1   D0    A2

        LONG GN_NTLMNegotiate(struct GNNTLM *ntlm, APTR out, ULONG size,
                              ULONG *length);

    FUNCTION
        Writes the first message ([MS-NLMP] 2.2.1.1), 40 bytes, and keeps
        a copy for the AUTHENTICATE message's MIC.

    RESULT
        GNERR_OK, GNERR_BUFFER, GNERR_STATE (called twice).

gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMAuthenticate     gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMAuthenticate

    NAME
        GN_NTLMAuthenticate -- answer a CHALLENGE_MESSAGE (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        result = GN_NTLMAuthenticate(ntlm, challenge, challengeLength,
        D0                           A0    A1         D0
                                     out, size, length)
                                     A2   D1    A3

        LONG GN_NTLMAuthenticate(struct GNNTLM *ntlm, CONST_APTR challenge,
                                 ULONG challengeLength, APTR out,
                                 ULONG size, ULONG *length);

    FUNCTION
        Reads the server's CHALLENGE_MESSAGE (2.2.1.2) and writes the
        AUTHENTICATE_MESSAGE (2.2.1.3) with an NTLMv2 response (3.3.2).
        The server's AV pairs are sent back with MsvAvFlags (MIC present),
        MsvAvTargetName and MsvChannelBindings (the configured channel
        bindings hash, all-zero when unbound), and the MIC is
        computed (3.1.5.1.2). The time is the server's MsvAvTimestamp when
        it sends one (then the LM response is Z(24)), else the Amiga clock.
        With KEY_EXCH a random session key is sent encrypted with RC4.
        Afterwards the context can GN_NTLMWrap() and GN_NTLMUnwrap().

        Whether the password was right is not known here: the server says.

    RESULT
        GNERR_OK; GNERR_PROTOCOL (a malformed challenge), GNERR_UNSUPPORTED
        (no UNICODE or no target info: NTLMv1 servers), GNERR_NORANDOM,
        GNERR_BUFFER, GNERR_STATE.

gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMWrap                     gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMWrap

    NAME
        GN_NTLMWrap -- seal and sign a message (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        result = GN_NTLMWrap(ntlm, in, inLength, out, size, length)
        D0                   A0    A1  D0        A2   D1    A3

        LONG GN_NTLMWrap(struct GNNTLM *ntlm, CONST_APTR in, ULONG inLength,
                         APTR out, ULONG size, ULONG *length);

    FUNCTION
        GSS_WrapEx() ([MS-NLMP] 3.4.6) with extended session security:
        out is the 16-byte signature (3.4.4.2) followed by the sealed
        message, inLength + 16 bytes. Each call uses the next sequence
        number, from 0.

    RESULT
        GNERR_OK, GNERR_BUFFER, GNERR_STATE (before authentication),
        GNERR_UNSUPPORTED (no extended session security).

gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMUnwrap                 gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMUnwrap

    NAME
        GN_NTLMUnwrap -- unseal and verify a message from the server (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        result = GN_NTLMUnwrap(ntlm, in, inLength, out, size, length)
        D0                     A0    A1  D0        A2   D1    A3

        LONG GN_NTLMUnwrap(struct GNNTLM *ntlm, CONST_APTR in,
                           ULONG inLength, APTR out, ULONG size,
                           ULONG *length);

    FUNCTION
        GSS_UnwrapEx() (3.4.7): the reverse of GN_NTLMWrap() with the
        server-to-client keys and the server's sequence numbers.

    RESULT
        GNERR_OK; GNERR_INTEGRITY when the signature or sequence number is
        wrong (out is cleared), GNERR_PROTOCOL, GNERR_BUFFER, GNERR_STATE.

gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMGetAttr               gfopenntlm.library/GN_NTLMGetAttr

    NAME
        GN_NTLMGetAttr -- read a value from an NTLM context (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        value = GN_NTLMGetAttr(ntlm, attr)
        D0                     A0    D0

        ULONG GN_NTLMGetAttr(struct GNNTLM *ntlm, ULONG attr);

    INPUTS
        attr - GNA_NTLMFLAGS (the negotiated flags), GNA_NTLMPEERVERSION
               (the CHALLENGE's Version field: major, minor and build as
               little-endian bytes 0 to 3), GNA_STATE (0 new, 1 NEGOTIATE
               written, 2 authenticated), GNA_CHANNELBINDINGS (1.1: 1 when
               bound). The CredSSP attributes read 0 here.

    RESULT
        The value, 0 for an unknown attribute.

gfopenntlm.library/GN_CredSSPNew                   gfopenntlm.library/GN_CredSSPNew

    NAME
        GN_CredSSPNew -- a CredSSP client over NTLM (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        credssp = GN_CredSSPNew(config, error)
        D0                      A0      A1

        struct GNCredSSP *GN_CredSSPNew(CONST struct GNCredSSPConfig *config,
                                        LONG *error);

    FUNCTION
        Makes a client for one CredSSP exchange ([MS-CSSP] 3.1.5) after a
        TLS handshake: NTLM in negoTokens, the TLS binding in pubKeyAuth,
        then the user's TSPasswordCreds in authInfo.

        PublicKey/PublicKeyLength is the server certificate's
        SubjectPublicKey: the contents of the BIT STRING in
        SubjectPublicKeyInfo without its unused-bits byte (for RSA, the DER
        RSAPublicKey SEQUENCE). ServerName gives the SPN TERMSRV/ServerName.
        MaxVersion is the TSRequest version offered, 2 to 6 (0 means 6); the
        exchange uses the lower of it and the server's. From 1.1,
        ChannelBindings/ChannelBindingsLength (and Flags
        GNF_CB_TLS_SERVER_END_POINT) give the NTLM exchange TLS channel
        bindings (see CHANNEL BINDINGS); an RDP client passes the server
        certificate's tls-server-end-point hash with the flag.

    RESULT
        The context, or NULL with *error set (GNERR_BADARGS without a
        public key).

gfopenntlm.library/GN_CredSSPFree                 gfopenntlm.library/GN_CredSSPFree

    NAME
        GN_CredSSPFree -- free a CredSSP context (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        GN_CredSSPFree(credssp)
                       A0

        VOID GN_CredSSPFree(struct GNCredSSP *credssp);

    FUNCTION
        Wipes the password and keys and frees the context. NULL is allowed.

gfopenntlm.library/GN_CredSSPStep                 gfopenntlm.library/GN_CredSSPStep

    NAME
        GN_CredSSPStep -- the next CredSSP message (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        result = GN_CredSSPStep(credssp, in, inLength, out, size, length)
        D0                      A0       A1  D0        A2   D1    A3

        LONG GN_CredSSPStep(struct GNCredSSP *credssp, CONST_APTR in,
                            ULONG inLength, APTR out, ULONG size,
                            ULONG *length);

    FUNCTION
        Call it first with in = NULL, then with each TSRequest the server
        sends, until it answers GNERR_OK or an error. Send out (length
        bytes) over the TLS connection after each GNERR_CONTINUE and after
        GNERR_OK.

          1. no input: TSRequest { version, negoTokens: NEGOTIATE }
          2. the server's CHALLENGE: TSRequest { version, negoTokens:
             AUTHENTICATE, pubKeyAuth, clientNonce (version 5 and up) }
          3. the server's pubKeyAuth, checked (the SHA-256 binding hash for
             version 5 and up, the public key + 1 below): TSRequest {
             version, authInfo: the sealed TSCredentials }; GNERR_OK.

        A TSRequest is a DER SEQUENCE; GN_CredSSPMessageLength() finds the
        end of one in a TLS stream. A buffer of GN_CREDSSP_BUFFER bytes
        always takes the output.

        After GNERR_OK an RDP client goes on with the next step of its own
        protocol (for HYBRID_EX, the Early User Authorization Result).

    RESULT
        GNERR_CONTINUE, GNERR_OK, or an error that ends the exchange:
        GNERR_SERVER - the server sent errorCode, an NTSTATUS
                       (GN_CredSSPGetAttr(GNA_SERVERERROR)), for example
                       0xC000006D STATUS_LOGON_FAILURE
        GNERR_PUBKEY - the server's pubKeyAuth does not match: a different
                       TLS key (a man in the middle) or a broken server
        GNERR_INTEGRITY, GNERR_PROTOCOL, GNERR_UNSUPPORTED, GNERR_NORANDOM,
        GNERR_BUFFER, GNERR_NOMEM, GNERR_STATE.

        A server that does not implement errorCode (CredSSP version 2)
        closes the connection on a failed logon instead.

gfopenntlm.library/GN_CredSSPGetAttr           gfopenntlm.library/GN_CredSSPGetAttr

    NAME
        GN_CredSSPGetAttr -- read a value from a CredSSP context (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        value = GN_CredSSPGetAttr(credssp, attr)
        D0                        A0       D0

        ULONG GN_CredSSPGetAttr(struct GNCredSSP *credssp, ULONG attr);

    INPUTS
        attr - GNA_VERSION (the version in use), GNA_PEERVERSION (the
               server's), GNA_SERVERERROR (its errorCode), GNA_STATE (0 to 3
               as the exchange goes, 9 after an error), GNA_LASTERROR (the
               GNERR_ code that ended it, as a ULONG), or one of the NTLM
               exchange's: GNA_NTLMFLAGS, GNA_NTLMPEERVERSION,
               GNA_CHANNELBINDINGS (1.1). GNA_STATE is CredSSP's own. The
               NTLM exchange inside a CredSSP context has no GNNTLM handle.

    RESULT
        The value, 0 for an attribute a CredSSP context does not have.

gfopenntlm.library/GN_CredSSPMessageLength
                                     gfopenntlm.library/GN_CredSSPMessageLength

    NAME
        GN_CredSSPMessageLength -- the length of a TSRequest in a stream (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        length = GN_CredSSPMessageLength(data, length)
        D0                               A0    D0

        LONG GN_CredSSPMessageLength(CONST_APTR data, ULONG length);

    FUNCTION
        From the first bytes of a DER message (at least 2; up to 5 for a
        long length), the whole message's length.

    RESULT
        The length; 0 when more bytes are needed; GNERR_PROTOCOL when the
        data is not a DER SEQUENCE.

gfopenntlm.library/GN_Digest                           gfopenntlm.library/GN_Digest

    NAME
        GN_Digest -- MD4, MD5 or SHA-256 (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        result = GN_Digest(algorithm, data, length, out)
        D0                 D0         A0    D1      A1

        LONG GN_Digest(ULONG algorithm, CONST_APTR data, ULONG length,
                       APTR out);

    INPUTS
        algorithm - GNALG_MD4 (RFC 1320), GNALG_MD5 (RFC 1321): 16 bytes
                    out; GNALG_SHA256 (FIPS 180-4): 32 bytes out

gfopenntlm.library/GN_HMAC                               gfopenntlm.library/GN_HMAC

    NAME
        GN_HMAC -- HMAC-MD5 or HMAC-SHA-256 (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        result = GN_HMAC(algorithm, key, keyLength, data, length, out)
        D0               D0         A0   D1         A1    D2      A2

        LONG GN_HMAC(ULONG algorithm, CONST_APTR key, ULONG keyLength,
                     CONST_APTR data, ULONG length, APTR out);

    INPUTS
        algorithm - GNALG_MD5 or GNALG_SHA256 (RFC 2104)

gfopenntlm.library/GN_RC4                                 gfopenntlm.library/GN_RC4

    NAME
        GN_RC4 -- RC4 in place, from a fresh key (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        result = GN_RC4(key, keyLength, data, length)
        D0              A0   D0         A1    D1

        LONG GN_RC4(CONST_APTR key, ULONG keyLength, APTR data, ULONG length);

    FUNCTION
        Encrypts (or decrypts) data in place. For NTLM's own use only; RC4
        is not a cipher for new protocols.
